%I A400102 #11 Sep 18 2026 16:48:55
%S A400102 9,91,4187,6533,8149,10001,11111,50851,79003,83119,94139,102173,
%T A400102 118957,148417,158497,166499,201917,226273,237169,287809,341503,
%U A400102 351809,413339,455971,463241,481601,491063,497377,520801,522349,632641,634351,642001,736291,903169,981317
%N A400102 Composite numbers k coprime to 10 such that the decimal expansion of i/k has the same period for i = 1..k-1.
%C A400102 Given base b and a number k such that gcd(k,b) = 1, the base-b expansion of i/k having the same period for i = 1..k-1 is equivalent to ord(b,t) = ord(b,k) for all divisors t > 1 of k, where ord(b,t) is the multiplicative order of b modulo t.
%C A400102 Let k = p1^e1*...*pr^er be the canonical factorization of k, then k has the desired property if and only if there exists some gcd(d,k) = 1 such that ord(b,pj^ej) = d for all j = 1..r. In particular, such k must satisfy b^gcd(p1-1,...,pr-1) == 1 (mod k), and any nonsquarefree k must be divisible by the square of a Wieferich prime in base b.
%C A400102 Any k with the property must be a strong pseudoprime in base b. Proof. We have ord(b,pj^ej) = d and d dividing pj - 1 for all j = 1..r, then it follows that d divides k - 1. If d is odd, the Miller-Rabin primality test will give the result 1 at an odd exponent. If d is even, then pj's are all odd, and we have b^(d/2) == -1 (mod pj^ej) for all j. This means that b^(d/2) == -1 (mod k), and so the primality test will give the result -1 at some exponent.
%H A400102 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400102/b400102.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1300</a>
%e A400102 91 is a term since ord(10,t) = 6 for the divisors t > 1 (namely t = 7, 13, and 91) of 91.
%e A400102 4187 is a term since ord(10,t) = 13 for the divisors t > 1 (namely t = 53, 79, and 4187) of 4187.
%e A400102 6533 is a term since ord(10,t) = 46 for the divisors t > 1 (namely t = 47, 139, and 6533) of 6533.
%e A400102 237169 is a term since ord(10,t) = 486 for the divisors t > 1 (namely t = 487 and 237169) of 237169.
%o A400102 (PARI) isA400102(n,base=10) = {
%o A400102 if(n==1 || gcd(n,base)!=1 || isprime(n), return(0));
%o A400102 my(f=factor(n), w=#f~, q=f[1,1]^f[1,2], d=znorder(Mod(base,q))); if(d%f[1,1]==0, return(0));
%o A400102 for(i=2, w, q=f[i,1]^f[i,2]; if(d%f[i,1]==0 || znorder(Mod(base,q))!=d, return(0))); return(1);
%o A400102 }
%Y A400102 Cf. A007732, A045616 (Wieferich primes in base 10). Subsequence of A020236.
%K A400102 nonn,base,new
%O A400102 1,1
%A A400102 _Jianing Song_, Sep 18 2026
